This peer develops Scott's dependent-product construction for polymorphic types. Its central result is that the function-space operator obtained by decoding fibre codes is a finitary projection.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`PolymorphicType`).** A code assignment \(d:U\to U\) is polymorphic when it is unchanged by projecting both its argument and its value to the universal subdomain of type codes: \(\operatorname{sub}\circ d\circ\operatorname{sub}=d\).

**Definition (`scFamily`).** For \(t\in U\), the Scott family consists of valid neighbourhoods \(T\) which contain \(t\) and are self-related by the universal subprojection.

**Theorem (`instNonemptyScFamily`).** The Scott family of every element is nonempty.

*Proof.* The master neighbourhood contains every element and is related to itself by every approximable map. ∎

**Definition (`scPrincipal`).** Each member \(T\) of the Scott family determines the principal element generated by the evidence that \(t\) belongs to \(T\).

**Theorem (`toElementMap_mem_fix_of_isRetraction_sandwich`).** If \(a\) is a retraction, every value of the sandwich \(a\circ g\circ a\) is fixed by \(a\).

*Proof.* Applying \(a\) once more introduces \(a\circ a\), which contracts to \(a\) by idempotence. ∎

**Theorem (`polymorphicType_apply_mem_fix`).** Every value of a polymorphic code assignment is fixed by the universal subprojection.

*Proof.* The polymorphic sandwich equation identifies the assignment with a sandwich by the subprojection; the preceding retraction argument then applies. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_toApproxMap_jArrow_of_fix`).** If \(w\) is fixed by the universal subprojection, then the self-map decoded from \(w\) is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* The fixed-point equation transfers through decoding to the function-space subprojection, whose fixed points are exactly the finitary projections. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_decode_subU`).** Decoding the subprojection of any universal element yields a finitary projection.

*Proof.* The image of a retraction is fixed by that retraction, so the preceding fixed-point criterion applies. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_piDUncurried`).** At a pair \((\varphi,t)\), the uncurried dependent-product operator decodes the projected code \(d(\operatorname{sub}(t))\) and applies that fibre projection to the value \(\varphi(\operatorname{sub}(t))\).

*Proof.* Evaluate the pairing, projections, and function-space evaluation in the defining composite. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_piDApply`).** For maps \(d,f:U\to U\), dependent-product application at \(t\) is the fibre projection decoded from \(d(\operatorname{sub}(t))\), applied to \(f(\operatorname{sub}(t))\).

*Proof.* This is the elementwise evaluation of the paired composite defining dependent-product application. ∎

**Theorem (`toApproxMap_toElementMap_piD`).** Decoding the image of a function-space element \(\varphi\) under the dependent-product operator gives dependent-product application to the map represented by \(\varphi\).

*Proof.* Currying identifies evaluation of the dependent-product operator at \(\varphi\) with the corresponding section of its uncurried form. ∎

**Theorem (`piD_rel_iff`).** A function-space neighbourhood \(X\) is related to \(W\) by the dependent-product operator exactly when both are valid and the section determined by the principal element of \(X\) belongs to \(W\).

*Proof.* This is the neighbourhood relation for a curried approximable map. ∎

**Theorem (`gSection_piDUncurried_rel_iff`).** The section determined by a valid function neighbourhood \(X\) relates a valid argument neighbourhood \(Y\) to \(Z\) exactly when applying the corresponding dependent-product map to the principal element of \(Y\) belongs to \(Z\).

*Proof.* The section relation is evaluation of the uncurried map at the two principal elements, and the explicit value formula identifies this with dependent-product application. ∎

**Theorem (`piD_toElementMap_mem_iff`).** A neighbourhood \(Y\) belongs to the image of \(x\) under the dependent-product operator exactly when \(Y\) is valid and the dependent-product application represented by \(x\) belongs to \(Y\).

*Proof.* Decode the image as an approximable map and use the membership characterization of its representing filter. ∎

**Theorem (`hii_easy_direction`).** If \(x\in X\), \(X\subseteq Y\), and \(X\) is self-related by the dependent-product operator, then the image of \(x\) belongs to every valid \(Y\).

*Proof.* The self-related neighbourhood \(X\) supplies the finite witness required for image membership, and upward closure carries it to \(Y\). ∎

**Theorem (`subU_principal_eq_of_rel_self`).** If a valid neighbourhood \(T\) is self-related by the universal subprojection, then its principal element is fixed by that projection.

*Proof.* The projection lies below the identity for one inequality; self-relatedness places the principal element below its projected image for the reverse inequality. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_rel_self_subset_of_mem`).** If \(t\) is fixed by the universal subprojection and \(t\in T_0\), then there is a self-related neighbourhood \(T\) containing \(t\) with \(T\subseteq T_0\).

*Proof.* Apply the finite-neighbourhood formula for the finitary universal subprojection to the membership of its fixed value in \(T_0\). ∎

**Theorem (`scFamily_directed`).** The principal elements indexed by the Scott family of a fixed point \(t\) form a directed family.

*Proof.* Intersect the two given neighbourhoods, refine that intersection to a self-related neighbourhood containing \(t\), and use inclusion reversal for principal elements. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_iSupDirected_scPrincipal`).** A fixed point \(t\) of the universal subprojection is the directed supremum of the principal elements indexed by its Scott family.

*Proof.* Every neighbourhood of \(t\) has a self-related refinement and therefore occurs above one indexed principal; conversely, every neighbourhood of an indexed principal contains \(t\) by upward closure. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_piD_of_formula`).** If image membership for the dependent-product operator is characterized by valid neighbourhoods containing a self-related finite witness, then that operator is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* The assumed equivalence is precisely the finite-neighbourhood criterion for a finitary projection. ∎

**Theorem (`le_idMap_piD`).** The dependent-product operator lies below the identity on the function-space domain.

*Proof.* Pointwise, each decoded fibre map is a projection and hence decreases its argument; projecting the input type code also decreases it, and monotonicity of the represented function completes the comparison. ∎

**Theorem (`piDApply_toElementMap_eq`).** Evaluating dependent-product application at the projection of \(t\) uses the same decoded fibre projection and the same projected argument as evaluating its explicit formula at \(t\).

*Proof.* Idempotence removes the repeated universal subprojection. ∎

**Theorem (`isRetraction_piD`).** The dependent-product operator is a retraction.

*Proof.* After decoding function-space elements, applying the operator twice repeats at each argument the same decoded finitary projection; its idempotence collapses the second application. ∎

**Theorem (`isProjection_piD`).** The dependent-product operator is a projection.

*Proof.* It is a retraction and lies below the identity by the preceding two results. ∎

**Theorem (`piDType_monotone`).** If \(s\leq s'\), then the fibre projection at \(s\) lies below the fibre projection at \(s'\).

*Proof.* The fibre-code map is monotone, and decoding function-space elements preserves order. ∎

**Theorem (`piD_rel_self_iff`).** A function neighbourhood \(X\) is self-related by the dependent-product operator exactly when \(X\) is valid and its associated section belongs to \(X\).

*Proof.* Specialize the general relation characterization to equal source and target neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_piDTypeMap`).** The fibre-code map sends \(s\) to the decoded function-space element represented by the projected code \(d(s)\).

*Proof.* Evaluate the composite defining the fibre-code map. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_piDType`).** Every fibre map selected by a code assignment is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* Its code is first passed through the universal subprojection, and decoding any such projected code yields a finitary projection. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_piDApply'`).** Dependent-product application at \(t\) is the fibre projection indexed by \(\operatorname{sub}(t)\), applied to \(f(\operatorname{sub}(t))\).

*Proof.* Substitute the elementwise formula for the fibre-code map into the explicit formula for dependent-product application. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_X_of_mem_step`).** If dependent-product application at \(x\) belongs to a one-step function neighbourhood from \(Y_0\) to \(Z_0\), then there is a function neighbourhood \(X\) containing \(x\), contained in that step neighbourhood, and self-related by the dependent-product operator.

*Proof.* Approximate the projected argument by the directed Scott family, use finitarity of the selected fibre projection to obtain a self-related output neighbourhood, choose a common upper approximation, and form the resulting step neighbourhood. Its defining relation gives self-relatedness. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_X_of_mem_stepFun`).** The same finite self-related witness exists when the target is a function neighbourhood formed from a finite list of step conditions.

*Proof.* Induct over the list. The empty list uses the master neighbourhood; for a new condition, intersect its witness with the inductive witness and use closure of the relation under intersection on the right. ∎

**Theorem (`hii_hard_direction`).** If dependent-product application at \(x\) belongs to a valid function neighbourhood \(Y\), then \(Y\) contains a self-related function neighbourhood \(X\) containing \(x\).

*Proof.* Every valid function neighbourhood is presented by a finite list of step conditions, to which the preceding finite-list construction applies. ∎

**Theorem (`hii_piD`).** The image of \(x\) under the dependent-product operator belongs to \(Y\) exactly when \(Y\) is valid and contains a self-related function neighbourhood \(X\) containing \(x\).

*Proof.* The forward implication combines the image-membership formula with the hard direction; the reverse implication is the easy finite-witness direction. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_piD`).** For every code assignment \(d\), the dependent-product operator determined by \(d\) is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* Its image-membership equivalence is the finite-neighbourhood formula required by the general criterion for finitary projections. ∎
